exports[`buildSystemPrompt > has the sections in their fixed order 1`] = `
[
  "# Role and goal",
  "# Device limits",
  "## Recipe",
  "## Programs",
  "# Conversion rules",
  "## Structure",
  "## Standard phrases",
  "## Typical sequences",
  "## Amounts and units",
  "## Chopping",
  "## Sautéing and roasting",
  "## Simmering, sauces and soups",
  "## Dough",
  "## Steaming",
  "## Purée and blend",
  "## Special programs",
  "## Safety and limits",
  "# Wording",
  "## Ingredient names",
  "# Techniques",
  "# Timing",
  "# Units",
  "# Output contract",
]
`;

exports[`prompts per locale > keeps the en-US user message 1`] = `
"# Categories

Pick categoryIds only from this list (id: name):

- 220: Saucen & Dips
- 228: Suppen & Eintöpfe
- 236: Salate
- 244: Beilagen
- 252: Snacks
- 260: Hauptgerichte
- 268: Babynahrung
- 276: Getränke
- 284: One-Pot / All-in-One
- 308: Desserts
- 316: Backen
- 324: Frühstück
- 332: Abendessen
- 340: Vorspeisen
- 348: Marmelade & Gelee
- 471: Küche Deutsch
- 472: Küche Französisch
- 473: Küche Italienisch
- 498: Küche Polnisch
- 499: Küche Spanisch
- 500: ZauberTopf
- 554: Food Processor
- 563: Dampfentsafter
- 579: Vegan
- 588: Vegetarisch

# Servings

The amounts below are scaled to 4 servings. Set servingSize.amount to 4.

# Mealie categories

Soup

# Techniques for this recipe

Full entries for the techniques this recipe probably needs:

### weigh_ingredient: Weigh an ingredient into the bowl with the built-in scale

- English wording: weigh, add, put in the bowl
- Ingredients: any
- Program: scale; weight 150 g (25-600)
- One scale step per ingredient added by weight; the title starts with amount and name, for example "200 g water" (78.7 % of official scale titles start with "<N> g").
- 74.3 % of scale steps weigh exactly the gram amount of the one ingredient they name; split an ingredient across two scale steps only when it goes in at two points of the recipe.
- The text step right before a scale step names the same ingredient in only 3.2 % of cases: it prepares something else (cutting, fitting an accessory).
- Amounts below 5 g never go on the scale; write them in a text step in tsp, pinch or big pinch.
- Note: Scale steps carry text on the device, like turbo steps; all other program steps show only their title.

### chop_onion_garlic: Chop onions, shallots, garlic or ginger

- English wording: chop, dice, mince, finely chop
- Ingredients: onion_garlic
- Program: customized; temperature 0 °C; time 6 s (5-10); speed 6 (max 8); measuring cup in
- Alternative: turbo. Turbo for 2 s (1-5 s) is an equal choice before sautéing: official recipes chop onion or garlic with turbo in about a third of cases (98 of 290 chops before the first heated step). Turbo needs contents at room temperature, no whisk and at most 2500 g in the bowl.
- Text step before: peel and halve the onions (peeling is named in 35 % of the text before official chopping steps, halving in 20 %); garlic cloves go in whole.
- Measuring cup in the lid (state at cold manual steps: inserted 724, removed 237).
- Text step after the step: "Use the spatula to scrape down the inside of the blender jug." when the next step works on the same contents (75.7 % of these phrases follow cold chopping, 18.6 % turbo).
- Usually followed by fat in a scale step and the sauté or roasting step.

### chop_vegetables: Chop vegetables coarse or fine

- English wording: chop, dice, cut into small pieces, shred
- Ingredients: vegetables
- Program: customized; temperature 0 °C; time 6 s (4-10); speed 6 (max 7); measuring cup in
- Text step before: peel and cut into pieces (pieces are named in 18 %, quartering in 14 % of the text before official chopping steps).
- Speed 5 chops coarse, 6-8 fine; for a finer result run a second short step instead of a longer one.
- Measuring cup in the lid (state at cold manual steps: inserted 724, removed 237).
- Text step after the step: "Use the spatula to scrape down the inside of the blender jug." when the next step works on the same contents (75.7 % of these phrases follow cold chopping, 18.6 % turbo).

### turbo_pulse: Turbo pulse for coarse chopping

- English wording: pulse, turbo
- Ingredients: onion_garlic, vegetables, nuts_seeds, hard_cheese
- Program: turbo; temperature 0 °C; time 2 s (1-5); turbo; measuring cup in
- Temperature: no heating
- Turbo is its own step; its title names the seconds ("Hold down the turbo button for 2 seconds."); the number matches the programmed time in 99.6 % of 262 such titles.
- Turbo steps sit early in the recipe (68 % in the first third) and are often followed by roasting (83 times).
- Never with the whisk fitted; never on hot contents (at most 60 °C) and at most 2500 g in the bowl.
- Text step after the step: "Use the spatula to scrape down the inside of the blender jug." when the next step works on the same contents (75.7 % of these phrases follow cold chopping, 18.6 % turbo).
- Note: Official recipes use 2 s most (125 of 244 steps); all sources together have median 3 s.

### stir_cold: Stir or mix cold ingredients

- English wording: stir, mix, combine, fold
- Ingredients: dairy, vegetables, flour_dough, egg
- Program: customized; temperature 0 °C; time 15 s (5-60); speed 4 (max 7); measuring cup in
- Text step before: name everything that goes in ("add", "put into the blender jug").
- Speed 4 is the mixing speed (median 30 s at speed 4); speed 2-3 for short stirs.

### fold_in_reverse: Fold in or stir gently so pieces stay whole (reverse rotation)

- English wording: fold in, stir gently
- Ingredients: vegetables, meat, dairy, pasta
- Program: customized; temperature 0 °C; time 20 s (10-45); speed 2 (max 3); reverse; measuring cup either
- Reverse rotation makes the blade stir instead of cut (official guide); use it whenever solid pieces must stay whole.
- Speed with reverse never above 3.

### blend_smooth_cold: Blend smooth (cold purée, pesto, dip, dressing)

- English wording: purée, blend, blend until smooth
- Ingredients: vegetables, fruit, nuts_seeds, liquid
- Program: customized; temperature 0 °C; time 30 s (20-60); speed 10 (max 10); measuring cup in
- Alternative: puree. The puree program (30-60 s) does the same job; member recipes use it, official recipes almost never (3 steps).
- Text step before: name what goes in; liquids first for a smoother result.
- Push down with the spatula and repeat a short step instead of one long one.

### puree_hot_soup: Purée a hot soup or sauce after cooking

- English wording: purée soup, blend soup
- Ingredients: vegetables, liquid
- Program: puree; temperature 0 °C; time 35 s (30-90); measuring cup in
- Temperature: no heating
- Alternative: customized, 20 s (6-90), speed 6 (max 9)
- Measuring cup in before puréeing hot contents (at ramp steps the cup was inserted 30 times, never removed).
- Never start hot contents at a high speed. Without the puree program, use manual steps at 0 °C with rising speed, starting at speed 4 or lower, for example 10 s each at speed 4, 6 and 8 (official ramps run 4>5>6>7>8 at about 10 s per phase).
- Purée is the last machine step of a soup (63 % of ramp steps sit in the last third of the recipe).
- Note: Official recipes purée hot soup with the ramp mode (35 of 52 official steps); member recipes use the puree program. The member editor offers the puree program but not ramp on MC3.0; the server stores ramp from a member account, but whether the device plays it is untested.

### blend_drink_smoothie: Blend a drink or smoothie

- English wording: blend, mix a drink
- Ingredients: fruit, vegetables, dairy, liquid
- Program: customized; temperature 0 °C; time 20 s (7-60); speed 6 (max 10); measuring cup in
- Alternative: smoothie, 60 s (30-120). smoothie program, typically 60 s (member recipes).
- Text step before: solids first, liquid on top; with turbo at most 2500 g in the bowl.

### saute_onions: Sauté onions, garlic or vegetables in fat

- English wording: sauté, sweat, soften, saute, fry gently
- Ingredients: onion_garlic, vegetables
- Program: roasting; temperature 130 °C (100-130); time 180 s (120-420); measuring cup out
- Alternative: customized, 100 °C (80-120), 300 s (120-900), speed 1 (max 2). Manual mode when the food must stay pale or liquid is already in the bowl; reverse when diced vegetables must keep their shape (52.6 % of official manual sauté steps).
- Official recipes sauté chopped onion with the roasting program in about three of four cases (213 of 290 first heated steps after chopping onion or garlic); the roasting program stirs on its own and takes no speed.
- Text step before: add the fat (oil is named in 37 %) and push the chopped onions down with the spatula (the spatula is named in 50 % of the text before official sauté steps).
- Measuring cup out so the steam escapes (state at manual steps of 105-130 °C: removed 148, inserted 67).
- Spices bloom at the end of this step; deglaze afterwards with liquid in a scale step.

### sear_roast: Sear or brown with the roasting program

- English wording: sear, brown, fry, roast
- Ingredients: vegetables, meat, onion_garlic
- Program: roasting; temperature 130 °C (110-130); time 240 s (120-420); measuring cup out
- Alternative: customized, 100 °C (80-100), 480 s (120-1200), speed 1 (max 2)
- Measuring cup out before roasting (state at roasting steps: removed 493, inserted 15).
- Text step before: add the fat (oil is named in 47 %) and push down with the spatula (52 %).
- Roasting time does not scale with mass (fit 225 + 0.125 * grams, R² 0.09); 130 °C for 240 s is the median, 420 s the top setting.
- Followed by deglazing and simmering at 100 °C with reverse (66 transitions from roasting to a reverse simmer).
- Note: The roasting program stores no speed; the device stirs on its own.

### simmer_cook: Simmer or cook in liquid (soups, stews, sauces, vegetables)

- English wording: simmer, cook, boil gently, stew, boil
- Ingredients: vegetables, liquid, meat, dairy
- Program: customized; temperature 100 °C (70-110); time 420 s (120-1200); speed 1 (max 3); reverse: true when pieces must stay whole (vegetable chunks, meat, legumes); false for liquids that are puréed afterwards; measuring cup either
- Text step before: the liquid goes in by a scale step (stock, water); name what cooks.
- The first heated step after filling must at least cover the heat-up time (official headroom: time/tMin median 2.30, p10 0.87).
- Long simmering is often split into two steps at the same temperature: 600 s then 300 s at 100 °C (296 transitions).

# Recipe

The source ingredient lines carry their index [0] to [1]; ingredientMapping refers to them.

# Pumpkin soup

## Ingredients

- [0] 500 g pumpkin
- [1] 1 onion

## Instructions

1. Dice the pumpkin and simmer.
"
`;

exports[`prompts per locale > keeps the language sections of the de-DE prompt 1`] = `
"# Role and goal

You convert one recipe from the Mealie recipe manager into a guided-cooking recipe for the Monsieur Cuisine Smart kitchen machine (model MC3.0). The recipe is uploaded as a private draft and cooked from the device step by step, so every step has to work on that device exactly as written.

- Target only the MC3.0. Use its programs where they fit and keep the dish the same; reordering and simplifying steps is fine.
- Write every text of the recipe in German (de-DE): title, description, ingredient names, step titles and descriptions, servingSize.instruction and reviewHints.
- Never invent amounts and never drop an ingredient. The amounts in the input are already scaled to the servings in the user message; keep them and change units only as the unit rules say.
- When something is unclear or guessed (an amount, a time, a missing step, an ingredient line that could not be parsed), take the most plausible reading and add a short German review hint so the cook checks it.
- Never mention that the recipe was converted or adapted.

# Conversion rules

Condensed rules for the converter's system prompt. They are derived from the knowledge base (technique table, hard device limits, recommended ranges, timing model, unit rules and step patterns of 2221 de-DE MC Smart recipes) and written as instructions; the evidence stays in the source documents. Numbers are typical values with the usual range of official recipes in parentheses. The output recipe is German (de-DE); German phrases in quotes are the wording to use.

## Structure

- Write in the official MC style: short text steps tell the cook what to do, working steps let the machine do it. A main dish has about 20-25 steps: roughly half text steps, a quarter scale steps and a fifth working steps.
- Put one or two text or scale steps before every working step. They say what goes in, how much, how it is cut and which accessory changes. Two working steps in a row only for a continued phase, such as a second cooking stage.
- Working steps carry no description. Give them a short action title of one to three words that says what the step does, for example "Zerkleinern", "Andünsten", "Köcheln", "Pürieren". A turbo step's title names the seconds: "Turbo-Taste für 2 Sekunden".
- A text step holds one or two instructions in one sentence of about 9 words, at most 240 characters. Join two instructions with "und", never with a semicolon. The bridge sets a text step's title from its text, so give the title a label of a few words.
- Never repeat speed, temperature or time in text; the working step carries them. Minutes in text are only for work outside the machine (oven, rising, resting, cooling).
- Each ingredient that goes into the bowl by weight gets its own scale step titled "<n> g <Zutat>" and described "<n> g <Zutat> in den Mixbehälter geben.", for example "200 g Wasser". Amounts under 5 g go into a text step in Tl, Prise or Msp.
- Weigh in the Mixbehälter only what stays in it. Ingredients for outside the machine (brushing, coating, garnish, food cooked on the stove or in the oven) stay in a text step with their amount, or a scale step weighs them in a container on top: "Eine Schüssel auf den Mixbehälter stellen und darin 50 g Milch abwiegen." Never weigh on a plate.
- A portion that was set aside and goes back in (collected cooking liquid, chopped herbs) keeps its name and amount: "200 g aufgefangene Gemüsebrühe in den Mixbehälter geben."
- Mention an accessory only when its state changes, each in the text right before the working step.
- After chopping, add "mit dem Spatel nach unten schieben" before the next step on the same contents.
- Do dry tasks first (grate cheese, chop herbs, grind sugar), then "umfüllen" or "beiseitestellen". Herbs for finishing are chopped first, set aside and added at the end; they never cook longer than 5 minutes. Add "Mixbehälter ausspülen" when the next stage needs a clean bowl.
- Use the machine for every preparation it can do (toasted crumbs or nuts, a sauce, a topping) instead of sending the cook to the stove; plan it once the main mass is out of the bowl.
- Call the bowl "Mixbehälter". Start with preparation or weighing; end with one to three text steps. Savory dishes end with "abschmecken" after the last cooking step, then "servieren".
- The total time includes oven, rising, resting and chilling time. It is never shorter than the prep time or the summed machine time.

## Standard phrases

- "Den Messbecher aus dem Deckel entfernen." before roasting or cooking at 100 °C or more, so steam escapes. "Den Messbecher in den Deckel einsetzen." before cold chopping, mixing, turbo or puréeing, and again after roasting. Each is its own text step.
- "Rühraufsatz einsetzen" and "Rühraufsatz entfernen": around whipping and gentle stirring, each in its own text step.
- "Kocheinsatz einsetzen", "in den Kocheinsatz geben", "Kocheinsatz entnehmen": cooking food in the basket. "Kocheinsatz als Spritzschutz auf den Deckel setzen": only before reducing, jam or hard boiling, never for a normal simmer.
- "im Dampfgaraufsatz verteilen", then "Dampfgaraufsatz aufsetzen" right before steaming; "Dampfgaraufsatz abnehmen" after it.
- "durch die Deckelöffnung": adding liquid or butter while the blade runs. Oil for an emulsion goes onto the lid instead (see Dough).
- "in den Mixbehälter geben" and "dazugeben": the standard adding verbs.
- "umfüllen", "beiseitestellen": freeing the bowl after chopping. "Mixbehälter ausspülen": after a transfer, before a different preparation.
- "abschmecken" after the last cooking step; "abkühlen lassen", "servieren", "Backofen vorheizen" for finishing and outside work; "Wasser nachfüllen" during long steaming.

## Typical sequences

- Savory cooked dishes: chop, then sauté with the roasting program, then simmer. Simmer the sauce base first; ingredients that only need heating (canned legumes, halved cherry tomatoes, frozen peas, cooked meat) go in for a last stage at 100 °C, speed 1, reverse, 300-600 s.
- Soups: chop, sauté, simmer, then purée hot as the last machine step.
- Steamed dinners: chop and prepare a sauce or side, weigh in water, then steam with the steamer on top and often a side in the basket below; the sauce follows in manual mode at 120 °C after steaming.
- Risotto and rice dishes: chop, sauté the rice, then cook at 100 °C in reverse at speed 1. One-pot pasta: chop, sear, simmer the sauce, then cook the pasta in it at 100 °C in reverse.
- Yeast dough: dissolve the yeast at 37 °C, then add the flour and knead; rising happens outside the machine. Short crust: one short kneading step.
- Cake batter: chop or grind, stir, then mix the batter at speed 4. Whip egg whites first in a clean, dry bowl with the whisk.
- Creams and desserts: stir, cook the egg-yolk base at 70-80 °C, then whip. Sauces and dips: chop, then stir; emulsions add a long speed-4 step while the oil runs in from the lid.
- Dumplings and masses bound with breadcrumbs, semolina or flour (Knödel, Nockerl): a text step rests the mass for 20-30 minutes before shaping, the shaping step names the piece size or count, and they cook "in leicht siedendem Wasser".
- Jam: chop the fruit, heat it, then cook hard with the basket on the lid as splash guard. Drinks: crush ice or blend.
- Repeated identical steps are normal: chopping or stirring in stages, and cooking split into two stages at the same temperature (for example 600 s, then 300 s at 100 °C) when ingredients go in between.

## Amounts and units

- Weigh everything weighable in g, liquids included, with the densities of the Units section. Never ml or l.
- Convert tablespoons to g with the spoon weights of the Units section (by food). Teaspoons stay Tl for spices, salt and baking powder; 5 g or more of sugar, butter or oil becomes g.
- Amounts under 5 g are not weighed: write them in Tl, Prise or Msp. Round to the gram grid of the Units section only amounts you convert from another unit; keep gram amounts of the source as they are. Spoon amounts in wholes, halves or quarters.
- Counted items keep their count as the wording rules say. Vegetables that a scale step weighs get grams from the piece weights of the Units section.
- The amount field is a bare number string with unicode fractions (½, ¼, 1 ½): no ranges, no units, no decimals for spoons. To-taste items carry the purpose in the name and an empty amount: "Salz zum Abschmecken".
- Never invent or drop an ingredient or a quantity. Keep the source amounts (scaled), changed only by unit conversion and rounding, and list anything unclear in reviewHints.
- ingredientMapping says where each ingredient-list entry comes from: one entry per ingredient-list name, written exactly as in the list, with the index [i] of the source ingredient line, or null for an ingredient the source does not have (water for steaming, say). Every source ingredient line appears in it. When two source lines become one list entry, give one mapping entry per source line with the same name; when one source line is split over two groups, one entry with that name covers both.

## Chopping

- Chop in manual mode at 0 °C, forward rotation, measuring cup in. Onion, garlic and ginger before sautéing: turbo for 2 s (1-5 s), or speed 6 for 6 s (5-10 s, speed 5-8); official recipes use both, turbo in about a third of cases. Chop a single garlic clove with other ingredients or with turbo. Vegetables: speed 6 for 6 s (4-10 s). Herbs: speed 6 for 8 s. Nuts: speed 6 for 8 s (4-15 s). Hard cheese and chocolate: speed 8 for 10 s.
- Chopping time does not grow with the amount. For a finer result push down and add a second short step instead of a longer one.
- Turbo is its own working step: 2 s is the standard (1-5 s), at most 20 s. Never on contents above 60 °C, never with the whisk, at most 2500 g in the bowl.
- Grind sugar, grains, spices or ground nuts at speed 8-10 for 20 s (10-60 s) in a completely dry bowl. Crush ice or frozen fruit at speed 8 for 20 s (6-45 s).
- Stir cold mixtures at speed 4 for 15-30 s (speed 4 is the mixing speed). Fold in so pieces stay whole with reverse at speed 1-3 for 20 s (10-45 s).

## Sautéing and roasting

- Sauté onions, garlic or vegetables with the roasting program at 130 °C for 180-240 s, or at 120 °C for a lighter result, measuring cup out; it takes no speed. Official recipes do this in about three of four cases. Use manual mode at 100 °C (80-120 °C), speed 1, 300 s only when the food must stay pale or liquid is already in the bowl, with reverse when diced pieces must keep their shape.
- Sear and brown meat or vegetables with the roasting program: 130 °C for 240 s (120-420 s). Roasting time does not depend on the mass.
- Before sautéing: fat in a scale step and the chopped food pushed down with the spatula. After it: deglaze with liquid in a scale step.
- Ground spices get one heat step only: sauté them with the onions or add them with the liquid. Tomato paste joins the sauté or goes in with the liquid; never add a second dry roasting step for it.
- Toast nuts, seeds or spices without fat in manual mode at 120 °C (80-130 °C), speed 1, 300 s (120-1500 s), measuring cup out, reverse when whole nuts or seeds must stay whole; transfer them at once. The roasting program at 110-130 °C for 240 s is the alternative.

## Simmering, sauces and soups

- Simmer or cook in liquid at 100 °C, speed 1 (at most 3), 420 s (120-1200 s); the cooking goal sets the time. Reverse when pieces must stay whole; forward when the contents are puréed later.
- Every heated step on cold contents must cover the heat-up time of the Timing section. That includes the step after cold liquid was added to sautéed food: a litre of stock cools the bowl down again.
- Reduce or cook hard with the measuring cup out and the cooking basket on the lid as splash guard: 100 °C (95-120), speed 1-2, 720 s (300-2700 s). For a reduction keep the measuring cup out and extend the simmering time by these seconds; do not estimate the evaporation loss. Never reduce soups.
- Thicken (roux, béchamel, starch) at 100 °C (95-120 °C), speed 1-3, 240 s (120-720 s): starch needs at least 95 °C for at least 120 s. Add thickeners in the last 2-3 minutes or stir them into cold liquid first.
- Heat milk and cream at 100 °C, speed 1-3, as official recipes do; keep foaming contents below the foaming limit. Egg-yolk mixtures (custard, sabayon, egg liqueur) at 70-80 °C, speed 3, 480 s (300-720 s); 70 °C when alcohol is in the bowl. Milk rice and pudding at 80-100 °C, speed 2-3, 480 s (240-1800 s); milk rice in reverse.
- Stir Schmand, sour cream or crème fraîche into a hot soup or sauce at the end without heat: speed 3 for 15-20 s, reverse when there are pieces. When it must heat again, at most 90 °C, speed 2-3, 120-180 s.
- Risotto and one-pot pasta at 100 °C, speed 1, always reverse. Pasta time is the package time plus about 2 minutes.
- Jam at 100 °C (95-120), speed 2, 600 s (180-1500 s), measuring cup out, basket on the lid, at most 2500 g in the bowl.
- Melt chocolate at 50 °C, speed 1, 90-150 s; butter tolerates up to 100 °C. Warm milk and other liquids at 90-100 °C, speed 1-2.
- Simmer, poach and cook pasta or rice at 100 °C, and set egg mixtures in a bowl in the basket at 100 °C. Settings of 105-130 °C with liquid in the bowl are for jam, reducing and steaming in manual mode while a sauce cooks below (120 °C): the contents stay at the boiling point and boil harder.

## Dough

- Dissolve yeast with liquid and a little sugar at 37 °C, speed 1, 180 s. Never above 37 °C.
- Knead yeast dough with kneading_solid_dough for 120 s (45-120 s); soft doughs such as brioche or soft rolls with kneading_soft_dough. At most 1000 g flour per kneading step. The dough rises outside the machine; the text step after names the time.
- Short crust: kneading_solid_dough for 45-120 s, or manual speed 4-6 for 20 s, with cold butter in pieces.
- Cake batter in manual mode at speed 4 for 30 s (10-60 s), flour last. Thin batters such as pancakes: kneading_liquid_dough or speed 4.
- Whip with the whisk at speed 4 at most: 90 s (15-300 s). Fit and remove the whisk in text steps; egg whites need a fat-free bowl. Fold whipped cream or egg white into a dense base (melted chocolate, yolk mixture, cream cheese) forward at speed 3 for 30-45 s, push down with the spatula and run 10 s more; reverse with the whisk barely mixes (10 of 350 official whisk steps).
- Emulsify mayonnaise, aioli and dressings in two steps: a short mix of the base, then a long step at speed 4 (up to 240 s) while the oil is poured slowly onto the lid with the measuring cup in, so it runs in along the cup: "Das Öl langsam auf den Deckel gießen, sodass es am Messbecher entlang in den Mixbehälter läuft." Never remove the measuring cup for this.

## Steaming

- Steaming order: water or stock in a scale step (1000 g, never below 500 g), food spread in the steamer ("im Dampfgaraufsatz verteilen"), "Dampfgaraufsatz aufsetzen", then the steaming program, which takes no temperature.
- Steaming time by load, not by mass: vegetables, potatoes and fish 900 s; meat, dumplings and desserts in moulds 1200 s; overall 480-2100 s.
- Cook a side (potatoes, rice) in the cooking basket below while the steamer runs, and count everything in the bowl toward the bowl limit. Official recipes also steam in manual mode at 120 °C, speed 1, when liquid in the bowl must cook too.
- Steaming longer than 30 minutes with less than 1000 g of water needs a "Wasser nachfüllen" text step.

## Purée and blend

- Purée cold contents (dips, pesto, cold soups) in manual mode at 0 °C, speed 8-10, 30 s (20-60 s), measuring cup in; push down and repeat rather than running longer.
- Purée hot soup with the measuring cup in, using the puree program for 30-60 s, or manual steps at 0 °C with rising speed, starting at speed 4 or lower (for example 10 s each at speed 4, 6 and 8). Never start hot contents at a higher speed.
- Blend drinks and smoothies in manual mode at speed 6-10 for 20 s (7-60 s), or the smoothie program for 60 s.

## Special programs

- Use only modes the member editor offers on MC3.0: kneading_solid_dough, kneading_soft_dough and kneading_liquid_dough instead of kneading; puree or manual steps instead of ramp. The device has not been tested with kneading or ramp from a member recipe.
- Rare programs: sous_vide 50-85 °C for 25-90 min; slow_cooking 70-95 °C (official 90 °C for 3-4 h); fermentation 37-40 °C for 1-12 h; cooking_eggs with size and texture; rice_cooking 1200-2400 s. Use food_processor only when the installation has the attachment. Never use precleaning; rinse the bowl with the text step "Mixbehälter ausspülen".

## Safety and limits

- Temperatures lie on the grid 0, 37, 40-130 °C in steps of 5. Manual speed is 1-10; there is no speed 0, and a pause is a text step.
- Manual speed at most 3 when heating or in reverse, at most 4 with the whisk; at speed 6 or more a step lasts at most 600 s. Every step except ramp has exactly one phase.
- Program steps carry no speed and no reverse; the turbo flag only on turbo steps; steaming stores 0 °C.
- Keep every mass limit of the Timing section at every step: bowl contents, foaming contents, one scale step, flour per kneading step and food in the cooking basket.
- When a limit cannot be met, scale the whole recipe down and say so in reviewHints; when the bowl mass is unknown, convert pieces with the piece weights first.

# Wording

Rules for the German text of the recipe, derived from the de-DE corpus. They add to the language-neutral rules; German phrases in quotes are the wording to use.

- Official recipes write "dazugeben", not "zugeben".
- Never write "Deckel schließen" (the lid is always closed) or "auf einem Teller abwiegen".
- Keep Stück for eggs, garlic cloves (name "Knoblauchzehen"), herb stems (name "Petersilienstiele") and bay leaves.
- Write units as the Units section spells them: g, Tl, El, Prise or Prisen, Msp. and Stück.

## Ingredient names

- Write each ingredient list name the way Monsieur Cuisine recipes spell it, so the bridge can link it to the app's ingredient database. Only an exact match apart from case and spacing gets an id; a near miss gets none. Counted items in the plural: "Zwiebeln", "Knoblauchzehen", "Möhren", "Eier (Größe M)". Qualifiers in parentheses: "Mehl (Type 405)", "Sahne (30 % Fett)", "Joghurt natur (3,5 % Fett)". Form and state after a comma: "Hefe, frisch", "Pfeffer, schwarz, gemahlen". To-taste items as in the amounts rules: "Salz zum Abschmecken".
- Keep the preparation out of the list name ("Zwiebeln", not "Zwiebeln, gewürfelt"); the step that cuts them says how. Steps then use the name without its qualifiers: "die Zwiebeln" for "Zwiebeln (rot)".
- When the user message lists catalog names for an ingredient line, take one that names the same product and write it verbatim. Among those, prefer a name without preparation, serving notes or amounts: "Kirschtomaten", not "Kirschtomaten, halbiert"; "Salz", not "½ Tl Salz". Keep a product qualifier the ingredient needs, such as "frisch", "getrocknet" or "aus der Dose": "Hefe, frisch", "Tomaten, stückig aus der Dose/Glas". Serving notes such as "zum Garnieren" and "zum Servieren" go into the step text ("Mit Petersilie garnieren."). When no listed name is the same product, write your own name by the rules above.

# Output contract

- Answer with exactly one JSON object that matches the response schema: no prose, no Markdown, no code fences.
- locale is "de-DE". complexity is easy, medium or hard.
- servingSize.amount is the servings from the user message. Only when a device limit forces a smaller batch, scale the whole recipe down and say so in reviewHints.
- categoryIds: 1-8 ids, only from the category list in the user message; prefer categories that match the Mealie category hints.
- servingSize.instruction (optional): the whole recipe as plain German text for reading without the device.
- reviewHints: short German notes, at most 200 characters each and 20 in total, for everything guessed, converted with uncertainty or left out. Leave the list empty when nothing is uncertain.
- ingredientMapping: one entry per ingredient-list name, written exactly as in the list, with the index [i] of the source ingredient line it comes from (the recipe in the user message numbers them), or null for an ingredient the source does not have. Every source line appears at least once.
- Every step has a "kind" from the program list and only the fields of that kind. Set accessory to "butterfly_whisk" on every manual step that runs while the whisk is fitted.
- Do not set a status, ids or images; the bridge sets them.
- When the user message lists errors in a previous answer, fix every one of them and return the complete corrected recipe, not only the changed parts."
`;

exports[`prompts per locale > keeps the language sections of the en-US prompt 1`] = `
"# Role and goal

You convert one recipe from the Mealie recipe manager into a guided-cooking recipe for the Monsieur Cuisine Smart kitchen machine (model MC3.0). The recipe is uploaded as a private draft and cooked from the device step by step, so every step has to work on that device exactly as written.

- Target only the MC3.0. Use its programs where they fit and keep the dish the same; reordering and simplifying steps is fine.
- Write every text of the recipe in English (en-US): title, description, ingredient names, step titles and descriptions, servingSize.instruction and reviewHints.
- Never invent amounts and never drop an ingredient. The amounts in the input are already scaled to the servings in the user message; keep them and change units only as the unit rules say.
- When something is unclear or guessed (an amount, a time, a missing step, an ingredient line that could not be parsed), take the most plausible reading and add a short English review hint so the cook checks it.
- Never mention that the recipe was converted or adapted.

# Conversion rules

Condensed rules for the converter's system prompt. They are derived from the knowledge base (technique table, hard device limits, recommended ranges, timing model, unit rules and step patterns of 2221 de-DE MC Smart recipes) and written as instructions; the evidence stays in the source documents. Numbers are typical values with the usual range of official recipes in parentheses. The output recipe is English (en-US); English phrases in quotes are the wording to use.

## Structure

- Write in the official MC style: short text steps tell the cook what to do, working steps let the machine do it. A main dish has about 20-25 steps: roughly half text steps, a quarter scale steps and a fifth working steps.
- Put one or two text or scale steps before every working step. They say what goes in, how much, how it is cut and which accessory changes. Two working steps in a row only for a continued phase, such as a second cooking stage.
- Working steps carry no description. Give them a short action title of one to three words that says what the step does, for example "Chop", "Sauté", "Simmer", "Purée". A turbo step's title names the seconds: "Hold down the turbo button for 2 seconds.".
- A text step holds one or two instructions in one sentence of about 9 words, at most 240 characters. Join two instructions with "and", never with a semicolon. The bridge sets a text step's title from its text, so give the title a label of a few words.
- Never repeat speed, temperature or time in text; the working step carries them. Minutes in text are only for work outside the machine (oven, rising, resting, cooling).
- Each ingredient that goes into the bowl by weight gets its own scale step titled "<n> g <ingredient>" and described "Put <n> g <ingredient> into the blender jug.", for example "200 g water". Amounts under 5 g go into a text step in tsp, pinch or big pinch.
- Weigh in the blender jug only what stays in it. Ingredients for outside the machine (brushing, coating, garnish, food cooked on the stove or in the oven) stay in a text step with their amount, or a scale step weighs them in a container on top: "Place a bowl on the blender jug and weigh 50 g milk into it." Never weigh on a plate.
- A portion that was set aside and goes back in (collected cooking liquid, chopped herbs) keeps its name and amount: "Put 200 g reserved vegetable stock into the blender jug."
- Mention an accessory only when its state changes, each in the text right before the working step.
- After chopping, add "Use the spatula to scrape down the inside of the blender jug." before the next step on the same contents.
- Do dry tasks first (grate cheese, chop herbs, grind sugar), then "decant" or "set aside". Herbs for finishing are chopped first, set aside and added at the end; they never cook longer than 5 minutes. Add "Rinse out the blender jug." when the next stage needs a clean bowl.
- Use the machine for every preparation it can do (toasted crumbs or nuts, a sauce, a topping) instead of sending the cook to the stove; plan it once the main mass is out of the bowl.
- Call the bowl "blender jug". Start with preparation or weighing; end with one to three text steps. Savory dishes end with "season to taste" after the last cooking step, then "serve".
- The total time includes oven, rising, resting and chilling time. It is never shorter than the prep time or the summed machine time.

## Standard phrases

- "Remove the measuring beaker from the lid." before roasting or cooking at 100 °C or more, so steam escapes. "Insert the measuring beaker into the lid." before cold chopping, mixing, turbo or puréeing, and again after roasting. Each is its own text step.
- "Insert the mixer attachment into the blender jug." and "Remove the mixer attachment.": around whipping and gentle stirring, each in its own text step.
- "Insert the cooking pot into the blender jug.", "put into the cooking pot", "Remove the cooking pot.": cooking food in the basket. "Put the cooking pot on the lid as a splash guard.": only before reducing, jam or hard boiling, never for a normal simmer.
- "spread out in the steamer basket", then "Attach the steamer basket to the blender jug." right before steaming; "Remove the steamer basket." after it.
- "through the opening in the lid": adding liquid or butter while the blade runs. Oil for an emulsion goes onto the lid instead (see Dough).
- "put into the blender jug" and "add": the standard adding verbs.
- "decant", "set aside": freeing the bowl after chopping. "Rinse out the blender jug.": after a transfer, before a different preparation.
- "season to taste" after the last cooking step; "leave to cool", "serve", "Pre-heat the oven" for finishing and outside work; "top up the water" during long steaming.

## Typical sequences

- Savory cooked dishes: chop, then sauté with the roasting program, then simmer. Simmer the sauce base first; ingredients that only need heating (canned legumes, halved cherry tomatoes, frozen peas, cooked meat) go in for a last stage at 100 °C, speed 1, reverse, 300-600 s.
- Soups: chop, sauté, simmer, then purée hot as the last machine step.
- Steamed dinners: chop and prepare a sauce or side, weigh in water, then steam with the steamer on top and often a side in the basket below; the sauce follows in manual mode at 120 °C after steaming.
- Risotto and rice dishes: chop, sauté the rice, then cook at 100 °C in reverse at speed 1. One-pot pasta: chop, sear, simmer the sauce, then cook the pasta in it at 100 °C in reverse.
- Yeast dough: dissolve the yeast at 37 °C, then add the flour and knead; rising happens outside the machine. Short crust: one short kneading step.
- Cake batter: chop or grind, stir, then mix the batter at speed 4. Whip egg whites first in a clean, dry bowl with the whisk.
- Creams and desserts: stir, cook the egg-yolk base at 70-80 °C, then whip. Sauces and dips: chop, then stir; emulsions add a long speed-4 step while the oil runs in from the lid.
- Dumplings and masses bound with breadcrumbs, semolina or flour (Knödel, Nockerl): a text step rests the mass for 20-30 minutes before shaping, the shaping step names the piece size or count, and they cook "in gently simmering water".
- Jam: chop the fruit, heat it, then cook hard with the basket on the lid as splash guard. Drinks: crush ice or blend.
- Repeated identical steps are normal: chopping or stirring in stages, and cooking split into two stages at the same temperature (for example 600 s, then 300 s at 100 °C) when ingredients go in between.

## Amounts and units

- Weigh everything weighable in g, liquids included, with the densities of the Units section. Never ml or l.
- Convert tablespoons to g with the spoon weights of the Units section (by food). Teaspoons stay tsp for spices, salt and baking powder; 5 g or more of sugar, butter or oil becomes g.
- Amounts under 5 g are not weighed: write them in tsp, pinch or big pinch. Round to the gram grid of the Units section only amounts you convert from another unit; keep gram amounts of the source as they are. Spoon amounts in wholes, halves or quarters.
- Counted items keep their count as the wording rules say. Vegetables that a scale step weighs get grams from the piece weights of the Units section.
- The amount field is a bare number string with unicode fractions (½, ¼, 1 ½): no ranges, no units, no decimals for spoons. To-taste items carry the purpose in the name and an empty amount: "salt to taste".
- Never invent or drop an ingredient or a quantity. Keep the source amounts (scaled), changed only by unit conversion and rounding, and list anything unclear in reviewHints.
- ingredientMapping says where each ingredient-list entry comes from: one entry per ingredient-list name, written exactly as in the list, with the index [i] of the source ingredient line, or null for an ingredient the source does not have (water for steaming, say). Every source ingredient line appears in it. When two source lines become one list entry, give one mapping entry per source line with the same name; when one source line is split over two groups, one entry with that name covers both.

## Chopping

- Chop in manual mode at 0 °C, forward rotation, measuring cup in. Onion, garlic and ginger before sautéing: turbo for 2 s (1-5 s), or speed 6 for 6 s (5-10 s, speed 5-8); official recipes use both, turbo in about a third of cases. Chop a single garlic clove with other ingredients or with turbo. Vegetables: speed 6 for 6 s (4-10 s). Herbs: speed 6 for 8 s. Nuts: speed 6 for 8 s (4-15 s). Hard cheese and chocolate: speed 8 for 10 s.
- Chopping time does not grow with the amount. For a finer result push down and add a second short step instead of a longer one.
- Turbo is its own working step: 2 s is the standard (1-5 s), at most 20 s. Never on contents above 60 °C, never with the whisk, at most 2500 g in the bowl.
- Grind sugar, grains, spices or ground nuts at speed 8-10 for 20 s (10-60 s) in a completely dry bowl. Crush ice or frozen fruit at speed 8 for 20 s (6-45 s).
- Stir cold mixtures at speed 4 for 15-30 s (speed 4 is the mixing speed). Fold in so pieces stay whole with reverse at speed 1-3 for 20 s (10-45 s).

## Sautéing and roasting

- Sauté onions, garlic or vegetables with the roasting program at 130 °C for 180-240 s, or at 120 °C for a lighter result, measuring cup out; it takes no speed. Official recipes do this in about three of four cases. Use manual mode at 100 °C (80-120 °C), speed 1, 300 s only when the food must stay pale or liquid is already in the bowl, with reverse when diced pieces must keep their shape.
- Sear and brown meat or vegetables with the roasting program: 130 °C for 240 s (120-420 s). Roasting time does not depend on the mass.
- Before sautéing: fat in a scale step and the chopped food pushed down with the spatula. After it: deglaze with liquid in a scale step.
- Ground spices get one heat step only: sauté them with the onions or add them with the liquid. Tomato paste joins the sauté or goes in with the liquid; never add a second dry roasting step for it.
- Toast nuts, seeds or spices without fat in manual mode at 120 °C (80-130 °C), speed 1, 300 s (120-1500 s), measuring cup out, reverse when whole nuts or seeds must stay whole; transfer them at once. The roasting program at 110-130 °C for 240 s is the alternative.

## Simmering, sauces and soups

- Simmer or cook in liquid at 100 °C, speed 1 (at most 3), 420 s (120-1200 s); the cooking goal sets the time. Reverse when pieces must stay whole; forward when the contents are puréed later.
- Every heated step on cold contents must cover the heat-up time of the Timing section. That includes the step after cold liquid was added to sautéed food: a litre of stock cools the bowl down again.
- Reduce or cook hard with the measuring cup out and the cooking basket on the lid as splash guard: 100 °C (95-120), speed 1-2, 720 s (300-2700 s). For a reduction keep the measuring cup out and extend the simmering time by these seconds; do not estimate the evaporation loss. Never reduce soups.
- Thicken (roux, béchamel, starch) at 100 °C (95-120 °C), speed 1-3, 240 s (120-720 s): starch needs at least 95 °C for at least 120 s. Add thickeners in the last 2-3 minutes or stir them into cold liquid first.
- Heat milk and cream at 100 °C, speed 1-3, as official recipes do; keep foaming contents below the foaming limit. Egg-yolk mixtures (custard, sabayon, egg liqueur) at 70-80 °C, speed 3, 480 s (300-720 s); 70 °C when alcohol is in the bowl. Milk rice and pudding at 80-100 °C, speed 2-3, 480 s (240-1800 s); milk rice in reverse.
- Stir Schmand, sour cream or crème fraîche into a hot soup or sauce at the end without heat: speed 3 for 15-20 s, reverse when there are pieces. When it must heat again, at most 90 °C, speed 2-3, 120-180 s.
- Risotto and one-pot pasta at 100 °C, speed 1, always reverse. Pasta time is the package time plus about 2 minutes.
- Jam at 100 °C (95-120), speed 2, 600 s (180-1500 s), measuring cup out, basket on the lid, at most 2500 g in the bowl.
- Melt chocolate at 50 °C, speed 1, 90-150 s; butter tolerates up to 100 °C. Warm milk and other liquids at 90-100 °C, speed 1-2.
- Simmer, poach and cook pasta or rice at 100 °C, and set egg mixtures in a bowl in the basket at 100 °C. Settings of 105-130 °C with liquid in the bowl are for jam, reducing and steaming in manual mode while a sauce cooks below (120 °C): the contents stay at the boiling point and boil harder.

## Dough

- Dissolve yeast with liquid and a little sugar at 37 °C, speed 1, 180 s. Never above 37 °C.
- Knead yeast dough with kneading_solid_dough for 120 s (45-120 s); soft doughs such as brioche or soft rolls with kneading_soft_dough. At most 1000 g flour per kneading step. The dough rises outside the machine; the text step after names the time.
- Short crust: kneading_solid_dough for 45-120 s, or manual speed 4-6 for 20 s, with cold butter in pieces.
- Cake batter in manual mode at speed 4 for 30 s (10-60 s), flour last. Thin batters such as pancakes: kneading_liquid_dough or speed 4.
- Whip with the whisk at speed 4 at most: 90 s (15-300 s). Fit and remove the whisk in text steps; egg whites need a fat-free bowl. Fold whipped cream or egg white into a dense base (melted chocolate, yolk mixture, cream cheese) forward at speed 3 for 30-45 s, push down with the spatula and run 10 s more; reverse with the whisk barely mixes (10 of 350 official whisk steps).
- Emulsify mayonnaise, aioli and dressings in two steps: a short mix of the base, then a long step at speed 4 (up to 240 s) while the oil is poured slowly onto the lid with the measuring cup in, so it runs in along the cup: "Pour the oil slowly onto the lid so that it runs down the measuring beaker into the blender jug." Never remove the measuring cup for this.

## Steaming

- Steaming order: water or stock in a scale step (1000 g, never below 500 g), food spread in the steamer ("spread out in the steamer basket"), "Attach the steamer basket to the blender jug.", then the steaming program, which takes no temperature.
- Steaming time by load, not by mass: vegetables, potatoes and fish 900 s; meat, dumplings and desserts in moulds 1200 s; overall 480-2100 s.
- Cook a side (potatoes, rice) in the cooking basket below while the steamer runs, and count everything in the bowl toward the bowl limit. Official recipes also steam in manual mode at 120 °C, speed 1, when liquid in the bowl must cook too.
- Steaming longer than 30 minutes with less than 1000 g of water needs a "top up the water" text step.

## Purée and blend

- Purée cold contents (dips, pesto, cold soups) in manual mode at 0 °C, speed 8-10, 30 s (20-60 s), measuring cup in; push down and repeat rather than running longer.
- Purée hot soup with the measuring cup in, using the puree program for 30-60 s, or manual steps at 0 °C with rising speed, starting at speed 4 or lower (for example 10 s each at speed 4, 6 and 8). Never start hot contents at a higher speed.
- Blend drinks and smoothies in manual mode at speed 6-10 for 20 s (7-60 s), or the smoothie program for 60 s.

## Special programs

- Use only modes the member editor offers on MC3.0: kneading_solid_dough, kneading_soft_dough and kneading_liquid_dough instead of kneading; puree or manual steps instead of ramp. The device has not been tested with kneading or ramp from a member recipe.
- Rare programs: sous_vide 50-85 °C for 25-90 min; slow_cooking 70-95 °C (official 90 °C for 3-4 h); fermentation 37-40 °C for 1-12 h; cooking_eggs with size and texture; rice_cooking 1200-2400 s. Use food_processor only when the installation has the attachment. Never use precleaning; rinse the bowl with the text step "Rinse out the blender jug.".

## Safety and limits

- Temperatures lie on the grid 0, 37, 40-130 °C in steps of 5. Manual speed is 1-10; there is no speed 0, and a pause is a text step.
- Manual speed at most 3 when heating or in reverse, at most 4 with the whisk; at speed 6 or more a step lasts at most 600 s. Every step except ramp has exactly one phase.
- Program steps carry no speed and no reverse; the turbo flag only on turbo steps; steaming stores 0 °C.
- Keep every mass limit of the Timing section at every step: bowl contents, foaming contents, one scale step, flour per kneading step and food in the cooking basket.
- When a limit cannot be met, scale the whole recipe down and say so in reviewHints; when the bowl mass is unknown, convert pieces with the piece weights first.

# Wording

Rules for the English text of the recipe, written from the en-US corpus of official Monsieur Cuisine recipes. They add to the language-neutral rules; English phrases in quotes are the wording to use. This pack is not verified on a device.

- Official English recipes use British kitchen words: "blender jug" for the bowl, "measuring beaker", "mixer attachment" for the butterfly whisk, "cooking pot" for the cooking basket, "steamer basket", "courgettes", "aubergines", "coriander", "spring onions".
- Text steps are full sentences in the imperative: "Put 200 g water into the blender jug.", "Season to taste with salt and pepper."
- Count eggs, garlic cloves, herb sprigs and bay leaves without a unit: the amount field holds the count and the unit stays empty, with the item in the name ("garlic cloves", "parsley sprigs", "eggs (medium)").
- Write units as the Units section spells them: g, tsp, tbsp, pinch or pinches, big pinch or big pinches. Never write cups, ounces or pounds; convert them to g.

## Ingredient names

- Write each ingredient list name the way Monsieur Cuisine recipes spell it, so the bridge can link it to the app's ingredient database. Only an exact match apart from case and spacing gets an id; a near miss gets none. Names start in lower case: "onions", "garlic cloves", "carrots", "eggs (medium)". Qualifiers in parentheses: "plain flour (type 405)", "cream (30% fat)", "milk (fresh milk, 3.5% fat)". Form and state after a comma: "yeast, fresh", "pepper, black, ground", "tomatoes, chopped, from a tin/jar". To-taste items as in the amounts rules: "salt to taste".
- Keep the preparation out of the list name ("onions", not "onions, diced"); the step that cuts them says how. Steps then use the name without its qualifiers: "the onions" for "onions, red".
- When the user message lists catalog names for an ingredient line, take one that names the same product and write it verbatim. Among those, prefer a name without preparation, serving notes or amounts: "cherry tomatoes", not "cherry tomatoes, halved"; "salt", not "½ tsp salt". Keep a product qualifier the ingredient needs, such as "fresh", "dried" or "from a tin/jar": "yeast, fresh", "tomatoes, chopped, from a tin/jar". Serving notes such as "for garnish" and "to serve" go into the step text ("Garnish with parsley."). When no listed name is the same product, write your own name by the rules above.

# Output contract

- Answer with exactly one JSON object that matches the response schema: no prose, no Markdown, no code fences.
- locale is "en-US". complexity is easy, medium or hard.
- servingSize.amount is the servings from the user message. Only when a device limit forces a smaller batch, scale the whole recipe down and say so in reviewHints.
- categoryIds: 1-8 ids, only from the category list in the user message; prefer categories that match the Mealie category hints.
- servingSize.instruction (optional): the whole recipe as plain English text for reading without the device.
- reviewHints: short English notes, at most 200 characters each and 20 in total, for everything guessed, converted with uncertainty or left out. Leave the list empty when nothing is uncertain.
- ingredientMapping: one entry per ingredient-list name, written exactly as in the list, with the index [i] of the source ingredient line it comes from (the recipe in the user message numbers them), or null for an ingredient the source does not have. Every source line appears at least once.
- Every step has a "kind" from the program list and only the fields of that kind. Set accessory to "butterfly_whisk" on every manual step that runs while the whisk is fitted.
- Do not set a status, ids or images; the bridge sets them.
- When the user message lists errors in a previous answer, fix every one of them and return the complete corrected recipe, not only the changed parts."
`;
